**Sec. 100001.** This section establishes that the fees in this subtitle apply to aliens in the described circumstances and defines terms using the Immigration and Nationality Act. It clarifies that references to sections or provisions within this subtitle are to the Immigration and Nationality Act unless otherwise specified.

**Sec. 100002.** The Secretary of Homeland Security or Attorney General must collect an asylum application fee of at least $100 in fiscal year 2025, adjusted annually for inflation. Fifty percent of fees from applications filed with the Attorney General go to the Executive Office for Immigration Review, while fifty percent from those filed with the Secretary go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. No waivers or reductions of this fee are permitted.

**Sec. 100003.** This section imposes a $550 initial fee for employment authorization applications by asylum applicants, parolees, and Temporary Protected Status (TPS) holders, with annual inflation adjustments. For asylum applicants, 25% of fees go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services for fraud detection, while all fees from parolees and TPS holders go to the Treasury general fund. No fee waivers are allowed for any of these groups.

**Sec. 100004.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $1,000 initial fee from aliens paroled into the United States, adjusted annually for inflation, with specific exceptions for medical emergencies, family reunification, and law enforcement cooperation. All collected fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted except for the specific exceptions listed.

**Sec. 100005.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $250 initial fee from aliens, parents, or legal guardians applying for special immigrant juvenile status, with annual inflation adjustments. All collected fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund.

**Sec. 100006.** This section amends the Immigration and Nationality Act to increase the Temporary Protected Status fee from $50 to $500, subject to annual inflation adjustments. All collected fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund, and no waivers or reductions are permitted.

**Sec. 100007.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $250 initial visa integrity fee from aliens issued nonimmigrant visas, with annual inflation adjustments. Fees may be reimbursed if the alien complied with visa conditions and departed within five days of expiration or obtained an extension or adjustment to lawful permanent resident status. All non-reimbursed fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund, and no waivers are allowed.

**Sec. 100008.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $24 initial fee for Form I-94 Arrival/Departure Record applications, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty percent of fees go to the Land Border Inspection Fee Account for U.S. Customs and Border Protection to process forms, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100009.** The Secretary of Homeland Security or Attorney General must collect a $100 annual fee for each calendar year an asylum application remains pending, with annual inflation adjustments. All collected fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund, and no waivers or reductions are permitted.

**Sec. 100010.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $275 initial fee for renewals or extensions of employment authorization for parolees, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty-five percent of fees go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100011.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a fee of not less than $275 for each renewal or extension of employment authorization for asylum applicants. Employment authorization terminates immediately upon denial by an asylum officer, 30 days after denial by an immigration judge without appeal, or immediately upon denial by the Board of Immigration Appeals. Twenty-five percent of fees go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100012.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $275 initial fee for renewals or extensions of employment authorization for aliens granted Temporary Protected Status, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty-five percent of fees go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100013.** This section imposes various fees for immigration court proceedings, including $1,500 for adjustment of status, $1,050 for waivers of inadmissibility, $500 for TPS applications, $900 for appeals of immigration judge or DHS officer decisions, $1,325 for practitioner disciplinary appeals, $900 for motions to reopen or reconsider, $600 for suspension of deportation, and $600 or $1,500 for cancellation of removal. Up to 25% of these fees may be transferred from the Immigration Examinations Fee Account to the Executive Office for Immigration Review, with the remainder going to the Treasury general fund. No fees may be used for the Legal Orientation Program.

**Sec. 100014.** This section amends the Electronic System for Travel Authorization (ESTA) fee structure to require a fee of not less than $10 for the system and not less than $13 per travel authorization. The authorization period for these fees is extended to October 31, 2034, with annual inflation adjustments for the per-authorization fee.

**Sec. 100015.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $30 initial fee for enrollment in the Electronic Visa Update System, with annual inflation adjustments. A new CBP Electronic Visa Update System Account is established in the Treasury, receiving all fees minus $5 per payment, which is deposited into the general fund. No waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100016.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $5,000 initial fee from aliens ordered removed in absentia who are subsequently arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, with annual inflation adjustments. Fifty percent of fees go to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for detention and removal operations, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers are permitted, except if the removal order was rescinded.

**Sec. 100017.** The Secretary of Homeland Security must collect a $5,000 initial fee from inadmissible aliens apprehended between ports of entry, with annual inflation adjustments. Fifty percent of fees go to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for detention and removal operations, while the remainder goes to the Treasury general fund.

**Sec. 100018.** This section amends the Immigration and Nationality Act to require aliens to pay fees for asylum applications, changing "may" to "shall" in the relevant provision. It clarifies that this does not limit the Attorney General's authority to set additional adjudication and naturalization fees under section 286(m).

**Sec. 100051.** The Secretary of Homeland Security is appropriated $2,055,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for immigration enforcement activities. Funds are designated for hiring CBP agents, removal operations, background checks, protecting alien children from exploitation, returning aliens from contiguous territory, state and local participation in security efforts, removing specified unaccompanied alien children, expedited removal of criminal aliens, gang checks for unaccompanied children, and IT investments.

**Sec. 100052.** The Secretary of Homeland Security is appropriated $29,850,000,000 for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Funds are designated for hiring and training personnel, providing performance, retention, and signing bonuses, recruitment, transportation, IT, facility upgrades, fleet modernization, promoting family unity for certain misdemeanor offenders, expanding 287(g) agreements, supporting the Victims of Immigration Crime Engagement Office, and hiring legal advisors.

**Sec. 100053.** The Secretary of Homeland Security is appropriated $750,000,000 for the Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029. At least $285,000,000 must be used for training newly hired federal and state/local law enforcement personnel, while no more than $465,000,000 may be used for facility procurement, construction, maintenance, and equipment.

**Sec. 100054.** The Attorney General is appropriated $3,330,000,000 for the Department of Justice for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Funds are designated for hiring immigration judges (capped at 800 by November 1, 2028), combating drug trafficking, prosecuting immigration matters, handling nonparty injunctive relief, increasing Byrne Justice Assistance Grant funding (excluding community violence prevention), maximizing lawsuit settlements, and compensating states for incarcerating criminal aliens.

**Sec. 100055.** This section establishes the "Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide (BIDEN) Reimbursement Fund" within the Department of Justice. Up to $3,500,000,000 is appropriated for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2028, for grants to states and local governments for locating, apprehending, detaining, and prosecuting criminal aliens, as well as for related law enforcement and court operations. Grants may cover activities occurring on or after January 20, 2021.

**Sec. 100056.** The Director of the Bureau of Prisons is appropriated $5,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. At least $3,000,000,000 must be used for hiring and training new employees and funding salaries and benefits for the current workforce, while no more than $2,000,000,000 may be used for facility maintenance and repairs.

**Sec. 100057.** The Director of the United States Secret Service is appropriated $1,170,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Funds may only be used for additional resources, including personnel, training, and technology, and for performance, retention, and signing bonuses for qualified personnel. Retention bonuses require a 2-year service commitment, and signing bonuses require a 5-year service commitment.

**Sec. 100101.** The Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts is appropriated $1,250,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028. These funds are for continuing analyses and reporting on court dockets, including assessing the number, frequency, and cost impact of judicial orders issuing non-party relief against the Federal Government.

**Sec. 100102.** The Director of the Federal Judicial Center is appropriated $1,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028. These funds are for implementing continuing education and training programs for judicial branch personnel, including training on the lack of authority for legal claims seeking non-party relief against the Federal Government and strategies to mitigate their cost impact.

**Sec. 100201.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to extend the termination date of the Fund to December 31, 2028. It also updates references to the end of the 2-year period to refer to this new date.

**Sec. 100202.** This section expands eligibility for claims related to atmospheric nuclear testing by extending the relevant period to November 6, 1962, and allowing physical presence in an affected area for at least 1 year. It increases compensation for leukemia and specified diseases to $100,000 and defines "affected area" to include specific counties in New Mexico, Utah, Idaho, Nevada, and Arizona.

**Sec. 100203.** This section expands eligibility for uranium mining claims by including core drillers and individuals involved in remediation efforts, and adding renal cancer and chronic renal diseases to covered conditions. It allows claims based on combined work histories in multiple positions (miner, miller, core driller, ore transporter) if specific exposure or duration thresholds are met.

**Sec. 100204.** This section adds a new provision for claims related to Manhattan Project waste, providing compensation of at least $50,000 or documented out-of-pocket medical expenses for living individuals who were physically present in specified ZIP codes in Missouri, Tennessee, Alaska, or Kentucky for at least 2 years after January 1, 1949, and contracted a specified disease. Surviving spouses or children of deceased claimants may receive $25,000.

**Sec. 100205.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to change the deadline for filing claims from "2 years after the date of enactment of the RECA Extension Act of 2022" to December 31, 2027. [end of text]


